$DIR/main.p:47:9: cannot return aggregateProject(h.b), which points into the object h refers to, which h keeps alive in siblingLeak
    aggregateProject(h.b) points into the object h refers to, which h keeps
    alive in siblingLeak. h (declared at main.p:45) is a Mixed: the object it
    refers to lives only while h holds it, and is dropped when h is dropped at
    the end of its scope in siblingLeak, or released when it is the last
    reference. A pointer into the object, or an interface that lends it, is a
    view that may not outlive h. When siblingLeak returns, h is dropped and the
    object freed, so aggregateProject(h.b) would point at freed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return h itself: an owned box moves to the caller, and a shared or synced
    one gives the caller its own reference:
        return h
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
